B-0 (Boosty) -- stdio input keeps musl's pushback invariant: fscanf works, and
getc stops costing a syscall per byte.

0002 replaced musl's readv-based __stdio_read with "a single SYS_read straight
into the caller's buffer" and called the dropped half "a throughput
optimization, not a semantic one". It was both, and the semantic half was
load-bearing:

  * __toread() parks the stream at rpos == rend == buf + buf_size.
  * Upstream's read then REFILLS f->buf and returns the requested byte from
    it, so the byte just handed out sits at f->rpos[-1].
  * The scan helpers lean on exactly that: shunget() is a bare `rpos--`, and
    __shgetc() stores the byte itself only when `rpos <= buf` (the unbuffered
    case, into the UNGET area).

With 0002 the byte went straight to the caller, rpos stayed at buf + buf_size,
and every shunget() stepped back onto buf[buf_size-1] -- a stale byte, usually
NUL. So on every real FILE, the scanf family failed at the first pushed-back
delimiter: fscanf(f, "%7s %d %d") over "alpha 12 -7" returned 1 with an empty
word. sscanf was never affected (a string pseudo-FILE has its own read). And
because the buffer never filled, every getc/fgets/getline byte was its own
SYS_read.

Found 2026-09-21 by the 0034 device pin: pouch-hello-malloc parses /ctl/memory
with fscanf and got 0. Ground truth before theory -- raw read(1), read(64),
fgetc and fread over the same file all returned the right bytes, which cleared
the kernel and devctl and left only the scan path.

The fix needs no readv. Upstream's one-byte-request arm on a buffered stream
was ALREADY a plain SYS_read into f->buf; this restores it verbatim. The bulk
arm (fread's remainder loop, and any unbuffered stream) stays a direct read:
fread never ungets, and an unbuffered stream takes __shgetc's buf[-1] path.
One SYS_read per call in both arms -- never a second read after a successful
first, which would block a pipe or tty that had already delivered what it had.

Behaviour change to know about: stdio input now reads AHEAD (up to BUFSIZ), as
it does on every other libc. A program that mixes FILE reads with raw reads of
the same fd, or hands the fd to a child mid-stream, sees standard POSIX
readahead instead of the accidental byte-exact positioning 0002 gave it.

Device-side pin (same commit, not in this patch file): pouch-hello-fopen gains
a `scan` leg -- three pushbacks deep, checked on VALUES -- measured RED on the
unfixed backend (n=1, w1=[]) before this patch was applied.

--- a/src/stdio/__stdio_read.c
+++ b/src/stdio/__stdio_read.c
@@ -1,17 +1,46 @@
 #include "stdio_impl.h"
 
 /* pouch: musl's __stdio_read issues one readv over {caller buffer, the
-   stream's prefetch buffer}; Thylacine has no readv. pouch does a single
-   SYS_read straight into the caller's buffer. The dropped half is the
-   readahead into f->buf - a throughput optimization, not a semantic one:
-   the stream simply refills on the next read. F_EOF / F_ERR are set
-   exactly as musl's original does (cnt < 0 -> F_ERR, cnt == 0 -> F_EOF). */
+   stream's prefetch buffer}; Thylacine has no readv. Two arms, ONE SYS_read
+   each -- never a second read after a successful first, which would block an
+   interactive pipe or tty that has already delivered what it had.
+
+   The refill arm is upstream's own (it never used readv there): a one-byte
+   request on a buffered stream -- __uflow, so every getc/fgets/getline/scanf
+   -- reads into f->buf and hands back the first byte FROM it. That keeps the
+   invariant the scan helpers lean on: after a read the returned byte sits at
+   f->rpos[-1], so shunget()'s bare `rpos--` re-reads it. 0002 read the byte
+   straight into the caller and left rpos == rend == buf + buf_size, so every
+   pushback re-read buf[buf_size-1] (stale, usually NUL): fscanf/scanf on a
+   real FILE failed at the first pushed-back delimiter, and every getc was
+   its own syscall.
+
+   The bulk arm (fread's remainder loop, or any unbuffered stream) stays a
+   direct read into the caller. It does not top the buffer up the way readv
+   did -- the next getc refills -- and needs no pushback slot: fread never
+   ungets, and an unbuffered stream takes __shgetc's buf[-1] path.
+
+   F_EOF / F_ERR exactly as upstream: cnt < 0 -> F_ERR, cnt == 0 -> F_EOF. */
 size_t __stdio_read(FILE *f, unsigned char *buf, size_t len)
 {
-	long cnt = syscall(SYS_read, f->fd, buf, len);
+	long cnt;
+
+	if (len - !!f->buf_size) {
+		cnt = syscall(SYS_read, f->fd, buf, len);
+		if (cnt <= 0) {
+			f->flags |= cnt ? F_ERR : F_EOF;
+			return 0;
+		}
+		return (size_t)cnt;
+	}
+
+	cnt = syscall(SYS_read, f->fd, f->buf, f->buf_size);
 	if (cnt <= 0) {
 		f->flags |= cnt ? F_ERR : F_EOF;
 		return 0;
 	}
-	return (size_t)cnt;
+	f->rpos = f->buf;
+	f->rend = f->buf + cnt;
+	buf[0] = *f->rpos++;
+	return 1;
 }
